# Acknowledged gitleaks findings.
#
# Every entry here needs a reason, and the reason must be that the value is NOT
# a credential -- never that rotating it is inconvenient. An ignore file used to
# quiet a real leak is worse than no scanning at all, because it makes the green
# build a lie.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8f46cee8 .github/workflows/ci.yml:148 -- generic-api-key
#
# A SHA-256 hash of the string "ci-e2e-key-not-a-secret", which was written in
# plaintext in the comment directly above it. The E2E suite needs a known API
# key so it does not have to scrape one from the server log, and the server
# stores only hashes -- so the workflow carried the hash of a deliberately
# public throwaway.
#
# It is not a secret in any sense: the plaintext is in the same file, in the
# same commit, and it grants access only to a server that CI starts and kills
# within one job. gitleaks flags it because `generic-api-key` matches any
# 64-character hex literal, which it cannot distinguish from a real token.
#
# The workflow no longer contains the literal at all -- the hash is now derived
# at runtime with `sha256sum`, which also removes the risk of the comment and
# the value drifting apart. This entry covers only the historical commit.
#
# History was NOT rewritten to remove it. Rewriting published history to purge a
# hash of a public string would break every existing clone for no security
# benefit, and the honest record is that this was committed and then improved.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
